  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 342 AM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nPersistent upper level ridging will flatten some and translate \neastward from the southern plains and more into the southeast part \nof the U.S. by Saturday into Sunday. Ensemble guidance continues to \nsuggests stronger winds aloft will develop southward from the \nnorthern plains as the ridge flattens. A disturbance embedded within \nthe flow aloft Wednesday afternoon...will likely bring a round of \nstronger and more widespread thunderstorm development across western \nNebraska. These will then push eastward across north central into \ncentral Nebraska Wednesday night. \n\nENS/GEFS mean PWAT values will be on the increase Wednesday night \nthough into the weekend as monsoonal/subtropical moisture aloft \nflows northward then turns eastward across the region on the \nnorthern edge of the upper high anchored to our southeast. Values \ngreater than 1.6\" appear likely which would be nearing or exceeding \nthe 90th percentile. As several waves aloft cross the area, several \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms will be likely Thursday into at \nleast the first of the weekend. Will have to monitor the potential \nfor some localized heavy rainfall with the abundance of atmospheric \nmoisture that will be in place. \n\nA considerable cool down is also expected Thursday into the weekend. \nThis is primarily due to abundant cloud cover and easterly low-level \nflow. But by Sunday, the low-levels begin to dry out some as Canadian \nhigh pressure settles southward into the plains. Temperatures are \nexpected to be much below normal during this timeframe, with highs \nin the 70s.",
